      "hints": [
        "Find the coordinates of $P(2, 3)$ and evaluate $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{2}{3}$.",
        "Find the perpendicular gradient $m_{\\text{normal}} = \\frac{3}{2}$.",
        "Form the normal line equation $y - 3 = \\frac{3}{2}(x - 2)$ and show it has $y$-intercept $0$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Evaluate the $y$-coordinate and derivative at $x = 2$:\n$$y = 2 + \\frac{3}{2(2) - 1} = 2 + 1 = 3 \\implies P(2, 3)$$\n$$\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{x=2} = -\\frac{6}{(2(2) - 1)^2} = -\\frac{6}{3^2} = -\\frac{2}{3}$$",
        "Determine the gradient of the normal to the curve at $P$:\n$$m_{\\text{normal}} = -\\frac{1}{-\\frac{2}{3}} = \\frac{3}{2}$$",
        "Form the equation of the normal line passing through $P(2, 3)$:\n$$y - 3 = \\frac{3}{2}(x - 2) \\implies y - 3 = \\frac{3}{2}x - 3 \\implies y = \\frac{3}{2}x$$",
        "Verify that the normal passes through the origin $(0, 0)$:\n$$\\text{When } x = 0:\\quad y = \\frac{3}{2}(0) = 0$$\n$$\\text{Thus the normal passes through } (0, 0)$$"
      ],
